# Agent Mode Rules

## Plan Mode Integration
When starting complex features (>3 files affected):
- Use Shift + Tab to activate Plan Mode before coding
- Let Cursor research codebase and identify relevant files
- Review the generated plan before proceeding to Agent mode
- This naturally complements `>om-plan` — use Plan Mode for Cursor-native research

## Parallel Agents (/multitask)
When `>om-cook` has 10+ independent tasks:
- Use /multitask to dispatch parallel subagents
- Each subagent runs in isolated git worktrees (no file conflicts)
- Pass full context in dispatch: task description, file paths, design spec excerpt
- Cap at 4 parallel agents per batch to keep resource usage reasonable

## Browser Verification
During `>om-check` and quality gates:
- Use Agent browser to open localhost and verify UI matches design spec
- Check browser console for JS errors
- Inspect network tab for API call verification
- Take screenshots for visual comparison

## Quality Loop (Cook-Check-Fix)
When executing tasks via >om-cook in Agent mode:
- After every 1/3 of total tasks, automatically run lint and tests
- If tests fail, attempt to fix automatically (max 3 attempts)
- If 3 fix attempts fail, STOP. Report the failure and suggest `>om-fix`

## Multi-File Edit Protocol
When a task requires editing more than 3 files:
1. List ALL files that will change with a one-line description per file.
2. Wait for user confirmation before starting edits.
3. Apply changes in dependency order: shared modules first, consumers last.

## Terminal Command Awareness
In Agent mode, prefer running tests and lint automatically instead of asking the user:
- Auto-run: lint, type-check, unit tests, dev server
- Warn first: git commit, npm install, file deletion
- Always ask: git push, git reset --hard, rm -rf, database writes

## Context Gathering
Before each task, use Cursor's built-in context:
- @Codebase to scan architecture
- @Files to read files relevant to the task
- @Git to check recent changes in the area
- @Docs to verify API/library usage
